From 8dde838e35138c7f53d10196ab2b8454fdd23510 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aeon <cshih@csie.ntu.edu.tw>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:25:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(transport): a multicast Join advertises the configured SN
 resolution

The multicast TX task built every Join with `resolution:
Resolution::default()`, while every receiver compares a Join's resolution
against its OWN configured one and ignores the peer on a mismatch
(`rx.rs`, "Unsupported SN resolution"). So any non-default
`transport/link/tx/sequence_number_resolution` gave a multicast transport
in which two identically configured peers never accept each other. The
SN masking already used the configured resolution; only the advertisement
was wrong.

It matters for the CAN link (nano-ros issue 1822). A QoS Join carries eight
PrioritySn; at the default 32-bit resolution a random SN is a 4-byte varint
and the Join is 99 bytes against a 63-byte CAN FD MTU. At 8 bits each SN is
at most 2 bytes and the worst-case QoS Join is 61 bytes (16-bit: 77). So an
8-bit frame SN is a config-only way to put a QoS Join in one CAN frame -
which this bug made unreachable.

Tests:
- multicast_resolution.rs: two UDP multicast peers at default and at 8-bit
  frame SN must meet within two join intervals. Without this fix the 8-bit
  case FAILS (8 s deadline, never meet); the default case is the rig control.
- multicast_can.rs: join_with_qos_fits_one_can_frame_at_8bit_sn measures the
  worst-case encodings (8-bit 61 B fits, 16-bit 77 B does not). The existing
  join_with_qos_does_not_fit_one_can_frame (default resolution) still holds.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
